package com.shahaabapps.core.content

/**
 * The AZ-204 episodes. Each one is drawn from its topic and says nothing the
 * topic does not: the same facts, the same examples, the same mistakes to
 * avoid, ending on the topic's own thing to try today. PodcastTest holds the
 * shape; whether a line is true is checked against the topic it came from.
 *
 * They are written for a beginner developer. Every Azure term is said in
 * plain words the first time it is heard, usually with an everyday picture
 * (the vault is a safe, a managed identity is a badge); Leo asks what a
 * beginner would ask; sentences are short, one idea a line; and the exam's
 * own phrasing is kept but flagged as such ("on the exam, if..."). Where the
 * five to seven minutes will not hold every detail, a detail goes rather than
 * the explanation. The Key Vault episode was the first written this way.
 */
internal object Az204Podcasts {
    private val appService = Podcast(
        "az-app-service",
        listOf(
            maya("Welcome back. Today it's App Service, which is Azure's \"just run my web app\" service. You hand it your code, or a container, and it gives you a URL, HTTPS, scaling and patching."),
            leo("And I never log into a server?"),
            maya("Never. There's no virtual machine for you to look after. [pause] The picture I like is a very well-run bakery. You bring the cake, which is your app. The bakery owns the ovens, the cleaning, the electricity and the front door."),
            leo("[chuckle] So what am I actually paying for?"),
            maya("A shelf. You decide how big a shelf, and how many copies of the cake sit on it. And that split is the first thing the exam tests, because every app has two parts."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("Part one is the App Service plan. That's the shelf. It sets the region, the operating system, Windows or Linux, the pricing tier, and how many worker instances you get. Part two is the web app, the cake. It holds your code, your app settings, your custom domain and certificates, and your deployment slots."),
            leo("Can two apps share one shelf?"),
            maya("They can, and they often do. Several apps can share one plan. But then they share its instances too."),
            leo("Ah. So if one app needs ten instances and the other needs one..."),
            maya("Then they need two plans. Instance count lives on the plan, not the app. That's a classic question. [pause] Okay, tiers, in one breath?"),
            leo("[laugh] In one breath. Ready."),
            maya("Free and Shared are for trying things out, on shared hardware, with no scaling. Basic gives you dedicated workers and custom domains, but you scale by hand. Standard adds auto scale, deployment slots and daily backups, and it's where most production apps live. Premium adds more power, more slots and network integration. Isolated gives you your own environment, for compliance."),
            leo("And the trick?"),
            maya("Slots and auto scale both start at Standard. So if a question asks for the cheapest tier that supports staging slots, the answer is Standard. Not Basic."),
            leo("Got it. Now, my app needs a database connection string. Where does that go?"),
            maya("Not in your code, and not in a settings file you commit. You add it as an app setting in Azure, and App Service injects it as an environment variable when the app starts. In .NET it simply turns up in configuration."),
            leo("What if my code reads a key with a colon in it? Like a section, then a name?"),
            maya("Good question. You write the app setting with a double underscore where the colon was. A colon isn't legal in an environment variable name on Linux, so the double underscore stands in for it, and ASP.NET maps it back to the colon for you. No code change."),
            leo("And the new value takes effect when?"),
            maya("Straight away, because saving an app setting restarts the app. [pause] Which is worth remembering. If your app sends reminders at a quarter past four, maybe don't change settings at a quarter past four."),
            leo("[chuckle] Noted. Are settings shared between slots?"),
            maya("They're per slot unless you mark one as a deployment slot setting, and then it sticks to its slot. The slots topic goes into that properly."),
            leo("What about logs? When something breaks?"),
            maya("App Service can write application logs, which is whatever your code prints, plus web server logs, detailed error pages and failed request traces. One detail: logging to the file system switches itself off after twelve hours, so it can never fill the disk by accident."),
            leo("So for anything long term..."),
            maya("Send the logs to a storage account, or to Application Insights, which the Monitor module covers. And to watch logs live from the shell, the command ends in log tail."),
            leo("Here's one I've hit. The app sits idle overnight, and the first request every morning is painfully slow."),
            maya("That's a cold start. The platform has unloaded your idle app and has to start it up again. The fix is Always On, which keeps the app loaded. It's offered from Basic upwards."),
            leo("Not a bigger instance?"),
            maya("No. A bigger worker makes a loaded app faster, but it doesn't stop an unloaded one having to start. And a second slot won't help either, that's a copy for staging, not a warm-up."),
            leo("Last piece. How does my code get there?"),
            maya("Push a zip, a Git repo, or a GitHub Actions workflow. Continuous deployment watches a branch and redeploys on every push. Or run a container from Azure Container Registry or Docker Hub. Deployment Center in the portal wires all of that up in a few clicks."),
            leo("Mistakes to avoid, then. Quick list?"),
            maya("Secrets committed in a settings file, use app settings or a Key Vault reference instead. Expecting slots on Basic. Forgetting that a settings change restarts the app. And on Linux, a key is read exactly as typed, case and all, so a setting that worked on Windows can come back empty."),
            leo("[sigh] I have definitely done the case one."),
            maya("[laugh] Everyone has. [pause] So, in three lines. A plan is the hardware and the tier, a web app is your code and its settings, and several apps can share a plan. Slots and auto scale need Standard. App settings become environment variables, and changing one restarts the app."),
            leo("And something to do today?"),
            maya("Fifteen minutes. On a free account, create a Linux web app on the Free tier, add one app setting, and watch the restart scroll past in the log stream. Then browse to the URL, and delete the resource group when you're done."),
            leo("Easy. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val blobBasics = Podcast(
        "az-blob-basics",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Blob Storage. It's where you keep files of any size: images, backups, logs, exports. And it's built in three layers. A storage account holds containers, and a container holds blobs."),
            leo("A blob being a file."),
            maya("A file. The account decides redundancy and performance, the container decides who can get in, and the blob is the thing itself. You pay for what you store, and for how often you touch it."),
            leo("Are all blobs the same kind?"),
            maya("There are three types. A block blob is the default, for files you upload whole or in blocks. An append blob is for logs, you can only add to the end, never edit. And a page blob is for random-access disks, it's what sits behind virtual machine disks."),
            leo("So an app writing a log line every second, never changing old lines..."),
            maya("Append blob. [pause] Now, redundancy. The letters look like alphabet soup, but they just tell you how many copies and how far apart."),
            leo("[chuckle] Alphabet soup is right. Start me off."),
            maya("LRS, locally redundant: three copies in one datacenter. It survives a disk or a rack failing. ZRS, zone redundant: three copies across zones in one region, so it survives a whole datacenter going down."),
            leo("And when the whole region goes?"),
            maya("GRS, geo redundant. That's LRS plus three more copies in a paired region. But you can only read the second region after a failover. If you need to read the secondary at any time, that's RA-GRS, read access GRS. And GZRS combines zones and geo copies."),
            leo("So the exam phrasing to listen for..."),
            maya("\"Must survive a regional outage and be readable from the secondary without failover\" is RA-GRS. \"Cheapest with zone resilience\" is ZRS. And plain GRS when it just has to survive losing a region."),
            leo("Okay. What about the tiers I keep hearing about? Hot and cold?"),
            maya("Access tiers. The idea is you pay either for storing a file or for reading it, never cheaply for both. Hot costs the most to store and the least to read, with no minimum stay. Cool is cheaper to store, dearer to read, with a thirty-day minimum. Cold is cheaper again, ninety days."),
            leo("And Archive."),
            maya("Archive is the cheapest of all, with a hundred and eighty days minimum. But here's the catch. [pause] An Archive blob is offline."),
            leo("Offline as in slow?"),
            maya("No, offline as in you cannot read it. Not slowly, not at a premium. First you rehydrate it to Hot or Cool, and on standard priority that can take up to fifteen hours."),
            leo("[sigh] Fifteen hours. So if a dispute needs a file from three years ago..."),
            maya("It's still there, still costing almost nothing to keep, but a read simply fails until it's rehydrated. Hot, Cool and Cold are all readable straight away, at different prices. Archive is the one that isn't."),
            leo("Who moves files between tiers, though? Surely not a person."),
            maya("A lifecycle rule. It's a policy that moves or deletes blobs by age. In the topic's example, a nightly export of study logs stays Hot for the current month, moves to Cool after thirty days, to Archive after a year, and is deleted after six years. Nobody touches it by hand."),
            leo("Nice. Anything that trips people up?"),
            maya("A few. Trying to read an Archive blob directly. Moving data to Cool and deleting it after a week, because the early deletion fee applies. Choosing GRS when the question says read access to the secondary, that needs RA-GRS. And creating an old version one account and wondering where the tiers went."),
            leo("Because tiers need..."),
            maya("A general purpose version two account, StorageV2. That's what you create today."),
            leo("[laugh] You've set up the ending."),
            maya("I have. [pause] Three lines first. Account, container, blob, with block blobs for files, append blobs for logs and page blobs for disks. Tiers trade storage price against access price, and Archive is offline until it's rehydrated. Lifecycle rules move and delete blobs by age, and the redundancy letters say where the copies live."),
            leo("And today's fifteen minutes?"),
            maya("Create a StorageV2 account with LRS. Upload a file to a private container. Change its tier to Cool in the portal. Add a lifecycle rule that deletes after one day. Then delete the resource group."),
            leo("Done before the kettle boils. Thanks, Maya."),
        ),
    )

    private val keyVault = Podcast(
        "az-keyvault",
        listOf(
            maya("Hi, and welcome back. Today is about keeping secrets safe, things like passwords, or the connection string your app uses to reach its database. Here's a puzzle to start. Your app needs a password to open a locked box. Where should the app keep that password?"),
            leo("In a settings file? [pause] Hmm, no. Then the settings file is the thing I have to protect. That just moves the problem."),
            maya("[chuckle] Exactly. And the surprising answer is, nowhere. The app doesn't keep one at all. We'll get to how. First, the box itself. It's called Azure Key Vault."),
            leo("What is it, in plain words?"),
            maya("A safe in the cloud. You put your sensitive things in it, and only the people and apps you allow can take them out. It holds three kinds of thing."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("First, secrets. A secret is any text you want hidden, like a password or a connection string. You can read a secret back, and the vault keeps the old versions when you change it. Second, keys. A key is for scrambling and unscrambling data, which is called encryption."),
            leo("Can I read a key back, like a secret?"),
            maya("No, and that's the important part. A key never leaves the vault. You send your data in, the vault does the scrambling inside, and sends you the result. The key itself stays locked up."),
            leo("Like a notary who stamps your papers but never lends you the stamp."),
            maya("[laugh] That's perfect. Third, certificates, the files behind the little padlock in your browser. The vault can even renew some of them for you, with a supported certificate authority."),
            leo("Are all vaults the same?"),
            maya("There are two tiers, which is Azure's word for price levels. Standard protects keys in software. Premium keeps them in special tamper-proof hardware, called an HSM. So on the exam, if the keys must be hardware protected, the answer is Premium."),
            leo("Okay. So who's allowed to open the safe?"),
            maya("You hand out roles. A role is a named permission, a bit like a job title. The recommended way is role-based access control, RBAC for short. For example, the role Key Vault Secrets User can read secrets and nothing more. Always give the smallest role that does the job, not Key Vault Administrator."),
            leo("And if someone deletes a secret by mistake?"),
            maya("There's a safety net. Soft delete is on by default, so a deleted secret goes into a kind of recycle bin for a while, and you can bring it back. Purge protection locks that bin, so nobody, not even an administrator, can empty it early. If the exam says prevent permanent deletion, think purge protection."),
            leo("Right. Back to the puzzle, then. How does the app open the safe with no password?"),
            maya("With a managed identity. Think of it as an employee badge that Azure makes for your app. The app shows its badge, and the vault checks whether that badge has a role. There's no password anywhere for you to store, leak or change. Azure looks after it."),
            leo("[laugh] A badge instead of a password. I like that."),
            maya("And badges come in two kinds. A system-assigned identity belongs to one app only. You switch it on with one click, and when that app is deleted, the badge goes with it."),
            leo("And the other kind?"),
            maya("A user-assigned identity. That's a badge you create on its own, first, and then hand to as many apps as you like. You give it its role once, and every app wearing it gets in."),
            leo("So if three apps need the same access, and it has to be ready before any of them exist..."),
            maya("Then it's user-assigned. A system-assigned badge can't be shared, and it doesn't exist until its app does."),
            leo("What does the code look like? Is it hard?"),
            maya("It's short. You use something called DefaultAzureCredential, and it's clever. On your laptop it uses the account you signed in with, by running az login. In Azure it uses the app's managed identity. The same code works in both places."),
            leo("And if I don't want to write any code at all?"),
            maya("Then use a Key Vault reference. It's a special value you put in your app's settings that says, fetch this from the vault. When the app starts, Azure fetches the secret for you, and your app just sees a normal setting."),
            leo("So with a reference I can skip giving out the role?"),
            maya("No, and that's a common trap. The badge still needs its role on the vault. The reference only saves you the code."),
            leo("[sigh] Okay. What do people usually get wrong?"),
            maya("Four things. Giving the app the administrator role when it only reads. Switching on the badge but forgetting the role, which gives you an access denied error, a 403. Pinning a reference to one old version of a secret, so updates never arrive. And deleting a shared badge while apps still use it."),
            leo("Can you sum it up in three lines?"),
            maya("One. Key Vault is a safe for secrets, keys and certificates, and keys never leave it. Two. A managed identity is a badge instead of a password, system-assigned for one app, user-assigned to share. Three. Read secrets with DefaultAzureCredential in code, or with a Key Vault reference in settings."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("Create a vault that uses roles, and add a secret. Give yourself the Secrets User role, and read the secret back from the command line. Then create a function app, switch on its system identity, add a Key Vault reference setting, and look for the green tick in the portal that says it resolves."),
            leo("Green tick. My favourite kind of tick. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val slotsAndScaling = Podcast(
        "az-slots-scaling",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, two ways to ship safely and handle the crowd. Deployment slots, and scaling. Start with slots. A deployment slot is a second copy of your app, with its own URL."),
            leo("A copy for testing?"),
            maya("For staging. You deploy the new version to the staging slot, test it there, and then swap it with production. Users never see a restart."),
            leo("How does a swap manage that?"),
            maya("Before switching anything, Azure warms the staging slot up by sending it requests. Then it switches the routing, so the staging workers start answering the production URL. [pause] The old production code moves over to staging."),
            leo("So if the new version is broken..."),
            maya("You swap back, in seconds, with no redeploy. That's the safety net. And notice what a swap is. It changes routing, not files. The workers that were serving staging, already warm, are the ones answering production afterwards."),
            leo("Here's what I always get wrong. My settings. Do they go with the code?"),
            maya("Most of them do. General app settings and connection strings travel with the code. But you can mark a setting as a deployment slot setting, and then it's sticky. A sticky setting stays with its slot."),
            leo("[chuckle] Sticky. I like that."),
            maya("The exam likes it too. If production must keep using the production database after a swap, you mark that connection string as a slot setting. If the new version should bring its new setting along, you leave it unmarked, so it moves."),
            leo("Anything else that stays behind?"),
            maya("Custom domain names and managed certificates stay with the slot. So do scale settings, IP restrictions and Always On. Handler mappings, public certificates and WebJobs content move with the code."),
            leo("And when do I mark something sticky? After the first swap?"),
            maya("Before. If you mark it after, the first swap has already carried the staging value across. [pause] And don't swap with a cold staging slot. Let the warm-up finish. If you use auto swap, which swaps as soon as a deployment to the slot finishes, it waits for the warm-up for you."),
            leo("Can I try a new version on just some of my users first?"),
            maya("You can. In the topic's example, they want ten percent of real users on version two for an hour. You set Traffic percent on the staging slot to ten. A user who lands on staging gets a routing cookie, so they stay on staging instead of bouncing between versions."),
            leo("And if I want to see version two myself, without moving anyone?"),
            maya("Open the production URL with a query string: x-ms-routing-name equals staging. After the hour, check failed requests per slot in Application Insights. If it's clean, set the traffic back to zero and swap."),
            leo("Nice. Okay, part two. Scaling."),
            maya("Two directions. Scale up means a bigger worker, more cores and memory, and usually a different pricing tier. Scale out means more workers behind the one URL, on the same tier, paying per instance."),
            leo("Which one do I want when traffic doubles?"),
            maya("If each request is the same amount of work as before, scale out. More of the same work needs more workers. Scaling up is for when one request needs a bigger machine."),
            leo("And there's a catch with scaling out, I'm guessing."),
            maya("[laugh] There is. Your app has to be stateless, or keep its state somewhere shared, like a cache or a database. If it keeps sessions in memory, users bounce between workers and lose their session."),
            leo("[sigh] That one has bitten me. Can scaling happen on its own?"),
            maya("From the Standard tier, yes. Auto scale adds or removes workers by a scale rule. CPU above seventy percent for ten minutes, a queue longer than five hundred, or a schedule, like weekdays at eight in the morning."),
            leo("Where do those rules live? On the app?"),
            maya("On the plan, because the plan owns the workers. And always set a minimum, a maximum and a cool-down. The cool-down is the least time between two scale actions, so the rule doesn't flap."),
            leo("Flap?"),
            maya("Go out, come back in, go out again. That's also why scaling in should be gentler than scaling out. Out at seventy percent, in at thirty. Using the same threshold both ways makes it oscillate."),
            leo("Any example of a schedule beating a CPU rule?"),
            maya("Two Tracks gets a burst every evening at a quarter past four, Qatar time, when the reminders fire. A schedule rule adds a worker at four and removes it at six. That's cheaper than reacting to CPU after the spike has already started."),
            leo("[chuckle] Planning ahead. So, three lines?"),
            maya("A swap warms up staging and then switches the routing, and you swap back if anything is wrong. Sticky settings stay with their slot, everything else moves with the code. Scale up is a bigger worker, scale out is more workers, and auto scale rules live on the plan."),
            leo("And something to try today?"),
            maya("This one needs a Standard S1 plan, which the free credit covers for an hour. Add a slot named staging to a web app. Change the app's title in staging only. Swap, and confirm production shows the new title. Then swap back, and delete the plan."),
            leo("Swap, check, swap back. Easy. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val functions = Podcast(
        "az-functions",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Azure Functions. A function is a small piece of code that runs when something happens. An HTTP request, a timer, a new message in a queue, a new blob. You pay per run, and there's no server to manage."),
            leo("So what decides when it runs?"),
            maya("Its trigger. And here's the rule the exam leans on. Every function has exactly one trigger. One event that starts it."),
            leo("Even if it reads from one place and writes to another?"),
            maya("Even then. Those are bindings, not triggers. A binding is a declared connection to another service, so you don't write any connection code. An input binding hands you data. An output binding takes whatever you return and writes it somewhere."),
            leo("So a function that reads a Cosmos document and writes to a queue..."),
            maya("Has one trigger, plus an input binding for the Cosmos read and an output binding for the queue write. [pause] As for what can trigger one, the list is long. HTTP for APIs and webhooks. A timer for nightly clean-ups. A queue message for background work. A new blob, to process an upload. A Cosmos DB change. And Event Grid, Event Hubs or Service Bus for integration."),
            leo("Tell me about the timer. I hear the format is odd."),
            maya("It is. Most CRON formats have five fields. Azure's timer uses six, and the first one is seconds. It's called NCRONTAB. Seconds, minutes, hours, day, month, weekday."),
            leo("[sigh] Of course it's six."),
            maya("[chuckle] So, every five minutes is a zero in the seconds field, then star slash five in the minutes field, then stars for the rest. Put the star slash five in the first field instead and you've written every five seconds."),
            leo("Good trap. What does a real one look like?"),
            maya("The topic's example is the Two Tracks reminder sweep, at four in the afternoon, Sunday to Thursday. The timer is the trigger. A Cosmos query is an input binding that hands in today's sessions. And the return value is dropped on a queue by an output binding. There's no connection code at all, the names of the connection settings come from app settings."),
            leo("And then something else sends the notifications?"),
            maya("A second function, triggered by that queue. Both sit in one function app, on Consumption, and cost cents a month."),
            leo("Let's talk plans, then. Consumption first?"),
            maya("Consumption scales to zero, and you pay per execution. The price is a cold start, a delay while an instance loads before the first run after idle. And a single run is capped, ten minutes at most, five by default. No network integration either."),
            leo("So if I can't have a cold start..."),
            maya("That points to Premium. It keeps instances pre-warmed, runs for sixty minutes by default, or as long as you configure, and supports network integration. But you pay per core-second even when it's idle, with at least one instance."),
            leo("And the third one?"),
            maya("Dedicated, which runs on an App Service plan you already have. It scales like a web app, and the cost is the plan, not the executions. One catch. A timer won't fire unless Always On is set, because the host goes to sleep when it's idle."),
            leo("[laugh] Asleep on the job."),
            maya("Exactly. There's also Flex Consumption, the newer Linux-only plan. Think of it as Consumption with a pool of always-ready instances to soften cold starts."),
            leo("Any exam phrases to listen for?"),
            maya("\"Must not have a cold start\" or \"needs network access\" points to Premium. \"Cheapest for something that runs a few times a day\" points to Consumption. And \"runs for thirty minutes\" rules Consumption out."),
            leo("What about configuration? Where do settings go?"),
            maya("The host file configures the whole function app, logging, time-outs and queue batch sizes. The local settings file is for local runs only, it's never deployed, and in Azure the same keys live in app settings. And the runtime needs a storage account setting, because it uses storage for timers and locks."),
            leo("One app, many functions?"),
            maya("Yes, but they all share the same plan, runtime stack and settings. Each function still has one trigger, and any number of bindings."),
            leo("Mistakes to avoid?"),
            maya("A five-field CRON. Running a twenty-minute job on Consumption, which gets killed at ten minutes, so use Premium or Durable Functions. Committing the local settings file, which holds secrets. And expecting a timer to fire on Dedicated without Always On."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("One trigger per function, and bindings replace connection code for inputs and outputs. The timer's CRON has six fields, starting with seconds. Consumption scales to zero but has cold starts and a run limit, while Premium pre-warms and allows network integration."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Create a Consumption function app in the portal. Add an HTTP trigger function from the template, and call it from the browser with your name. Then add a timer function that runs every minute, and watch Monitor show the runs. Delete the resource group when you're done."),
            leo("A function that runs every minute. I'll try not to be hypnotised. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val durable = Podcast(
        "az-durable",
        listOf(
            maya("Here's a puzzle to start. A workflow waits three days for a person to approve something. What is the waiting code doing for those three days?"),
            leo("Sleeping in memory, I'd have said. [pause] But you're asking, so that's probably wrong."),
            maya("[chuckle] It's not running at all. And when it wakes up, it replays from the very beginning. That one fact explains almost everything about Durable Functions."),
            leo("Okay, back up. Why do I need them?"),
            maya("Plain functions are stateless and short. Durable Functions let you write a workflow that runs for hours or days, remembers where it is, and survives restarts. And you write it as ordinary code that looks synchronous."),
            leo("What are the moving parts?"),
            maya("Three kinds of function. A client, or starter, is any normal function that kicks off an orchestration and gets back an instance id. The orchestrator is the workflow itself. It calls activities in order, waits for them, and keeps the state. And an activity is one unit of real work. Call an API, write a row, send an email."),
            leo("So where does the real work happen?"),
            maya("In activities. Always. All the side effects live there. Which brings us back to the replay. Each time the orchestrator wakes up, it runs from line one, but it reads the results of finished activities from its history instead of running them again."),
            leo("Where's that history kept?"),
            maya("In the task hub, which is a set of storage tables and queues where the orchestration's state lives. [pause] Here's the topic's example. An orchestrator calls activity A, then sleeps for three days, then calls B. After A finishes, the host may unload everything."),
            leo("And three days later?"),
            maya("The timer fires, and the orchestrator replays from the top. A is called again, but its result comes from history in a millisecond. The timer is already satisfied. And the code reaches B for the first time."),
            leo("That's clever. But I can see a trap coming."),
            maya("The trap is that the orchestrator must be deterministic. Every replay has to make the same decisions. So no random numbers, no reading the current time, no network calls directly."),
            leo("Why not the current time?"),
            maya("Because DateTime.UtcNow returns something new on every replay, so the orchestration would take a different path each time. Same with a new GUID. If line one names an order with a fresh GUID, replay produces a different id, the history doesn't match, and you get a non-determinism error."),
            leo("[sigh] So what do I use instead?"),
            maya("The context gives you replay-safe versions. Read the clock with context dot CurrentUtcDateTime. And to wait, use a durable timer, created from the context with CreateTimer."),
            leo("Why not Thread.Sleep, or Task.Delay?"),
            maya("Thread.Sleep blocks the host. Task.Delay wouldn't survive the orchestrator being unloaded. And neither is recorded in the history, so on replay the wait would happen all over again. A durable timer is the only safe way to wait."),
            leo("Okay. What about a payment call? Somewhere in the orchestrator?"),
            maya("Never there. Put it in an activity. In the orchestrator it would run again on every replay. [pause] And one more thing about activities. They can run more than once, they're at least once, so make them idempotent."),
            leo("[laugh] Charging someone twice would be a bad look."),
            maya("It would. Now, the exam names five patterns, and you can tell them by shape. Function chaining is A, then B, then C, each using the last result. Validate, charge, email."),
            leo("Next?"),
            maya("Fan-out, fan-in. Start many activities in parallel and wait for all of them, with Task.WhenAll. Resize a hundred images, then build one gallery from the results. Then the async HTTP API. Start long work, hand the caller an immediate response and a status URL to poll. Report generation is the classic."),
            leo("And the last two?"),
            maya("Monitor is a loop with a durable timer between checks, until a condition holds or a deadline passes. Polling a job until it's done. And human interaction is waiting for an external event, with a time-out."),
            leo("That's the approval one."),
            maya("Exactly. \"Wait for a manager to approve within seventy-two hours\" is WaitForExternalEvent, raced against a durable timer. If the approval wins, grant it. If the timer wins, escalate. From outside, the manager's app raises the named event with a POST to the URL the client handed back."),
            leo("Mistakes, then?"),
            maya("Using DateTime.Now or a new GUID in an orchestrator. Waiting with Thread.Sleep or Task.Delay. Doing input and output in the orchestrator because it's quicker. And forgetting that activities can run twice."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("The client starts, the orchestrator coordinates, activities do the work. Orchestrators replay, so they must be deterministic, which means context time and durable timers. And know the five patterns by their shape."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("It works locally with Azurite, so no Azure resources at all. Create a project from the Durable Functions orchestration template. Start it and POST to the start endpoint. Open the status URL from the response. Then add a thirty-second durable timer, and watch the status stay Running, then turn Completed."),
            leo("Thirty seconds of patience. I can manage that. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val containers = Podcast(
        "az-containers",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, containers on Azure. A container is your app plus everything it needs, packed into one image that runs the same anywhere. And Azure gives you three things. A place to store images, a quick way to run one, and a managed way to run many."),
            leo("Three services, then. Start with storing."),
            maya("Azure Container Registry, ACR. It's your private Docker registry. You push images to it, and Azure services pull from it. It can also build images for you, with an ACR Task, so a laptop without Docker can still ship."),
            leo("[chuckle] Good news for my laptop. Are there tiers?"),
            maya("Three. Basic, with ten gigabytes, for learning and small teams. Standard, with a hundred, for most production use. And Premium, with five hundred, which adds geo-replication, private link and content trust."),
            leo("So if the question says geo-replication..."),
            maya("Premium, and only Premium. Starting on Basic and then needing geo-replication is one of the topic's mistakes. [pause] In the topic's example, an ACR Task watches the main branch on GitHub, and on every push it builds the API image, tagged with the run number and latest."),
            leo("Okay. Second service. Running one container quickly."),
            maya("Azure Container Instances, ACI. It runs a container the moment you ask, billed by the second, with no cluster to create. It's the simplest way to run a one-off job, a build agent, or a small always-on process."),
            leo("Can it run more than one container together?"),
            maya("Yes, as a container group. Several containers that share a host, a network and storage, like a tiny pod. And three settings matter. The first is the restart policy. Always, for services. OnFailure, for jobs that should retry. Never, for one-shot tasks."),
            leo("So a nightly export that should retry if it crashes, but not loop for ever once it succeeds..."),
            maya("OnFailure. It restarts only on a non-zero exit. Always would restart it after success too, and Never wouldn't retry at all."),
            leo("[laugh] An export that runs for ever. Expensive."),
            maya("Very. The second setting is files. Container disks are thrown away, so mount an Azure Files share for anything you want to keep. And third, secrets. Pass them as secure environment variables or a secret volume, never baked into the image."),
            leo("And the third service, for running many?"),
            maya("Azure Container Apps. It's the managed home for microservices. It runs on Kubernetes underneath, but hides it from you. You get scale to zero, HTTPS ingress, revisions, traffic splitting and Dapr building blocks, without touching a cluster."),
            leo("What's a revision?"),
            maya("Every deployment creates one, and a revision is immutable. In single revision mode, the new one replaces the old. In multiple revision mode you keep both, and split traffic between them by percentage."),
            leo("So that's how I'd put a tenth of my users on a new version."),
            maya("Exactly, and that was the warm-up question. Neither ACI nor the registry can do it. ACI's restart policy only says what happens when a container exits, and registry tags only name images. Revisions are Container Apps' answer to deployment slots."),
            leo("And rolling back?"),
            maya("Point traffic at the previous revision instead of the latest. The image is already in the registry, nothing needs building, and because a revision is immutable the old version is exactly what it was. A rollback is a routing change, not a build."),
            leo("How does it scale?"),
            maya("With KEDA, the scaler underneath. Rules on HTTP concurrency, which is the default, or CPU, memory, or an event source like a Service Bus queue length. Set the minimum replicas to zero to pay nothing while it's idle, or to one to avoid cold starts."),
            leo("[sigh] Cold starts again."),
            maya("[chuckle] They follow you everywhere. Leaving the minimum at zero for a latency-sensitive API means the first request after idle pays for the start-up. The Two Tracks API keeps a minimum of one, so there's no cold start when the reminders fire, and a maximum of five."),
            leo("How does the app pull from the registry? With a password?"),
            maya("No. With a managed identity, granted the pull role on the registry. No password stored anywhere. Storing the registry password in the app is another mistake on the list."),
            leo("And where does Kubernetes itself come in?"),
            maya("Azure Kubernetes Service, AKS. Your cluster, your YAML, your custom controllers, and the most work to run. Pick it only when the question demands full Kubernetes control. \"Run this container now, no orchestration\" is ACI. \"Microservices, scale to zero, revisions, Dapr\" is Container Apps."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("ACR stores images and can build them in the cloud with ACR Tasks. ACI runs a container or a group by the second, where the restart policy and Azure Files mounts matter. And Container Apps adds revisions, traffic splitting, KEDA scaling and scale to zero, without managing Kubernetes."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("A few cents' worth. Create a Basic registry. Build a hello-world Dockerfile into it with the registry's own build command. Create a container instance from that image with the restart policy Never, and read its logs to see the output. Then delete the resource group."),
            leo("Hello, world, from the cloud. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val apim = Podcast(
        "az-apim",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, API Management. One front door for all your APIs. It sits between your callers and your back-end APIs, and gives every API one address."),
            leo("What does the front door actually do?"),
            maya("Quite a lot. It checks keys and tokens. It limits how often a caller may hit you. It rewrites requests and responses. And it publishes a developer portal, with documentation and a try-it page."),
            leo("Who does the checking, exactly?"),
            maya("The gateway. That's the part that receives every call and runs your policies. Separately there's the management plane, which configures everything, and the developer portal, which documents it."),
            leo("And the vocabulary? I keep tripping on products and subscriptions."),
            maya("An API is a set of operations mapped to a back end. An operation is one method and URL template. A product is a bundle of APIs offered to developers, with terms and quotas. And a subscription is a pair of keys granting access to a product or API."),
            leo("Where does the key go in the request?"),
            maya("In a header, the subscription key header, or in the query string. But note what that key proves. It authenticates the caller to API Management. Not API Management to your back end. That's a separate step."),
            leo("[chuckle] Two different handshakes."),
            maya("Exactly. Now, tiers, briefly. Consumption is serverless and pay per call, it starts fast, but it has no developer portal and no network integration. Developer is for non-production, with no SLA. Basic and Standard are for production. And Premium adds multiple regions, network injection and the self-hosted gateway."),
            leo("Self-hosted gateway?"),
            maya("The gateway as a container you run on your own infrastructure. If a question says the gateway must run on-premises, or in another cloud, that's the self-hosted gateway, which needs Premium, or Developer for testing. And multi-region active deployment is Premium too."),
            leo("Okay. Policies. You said that's where the exam lives."),
            maya("It really is. A policy is an XML statement the gateway runs against a call, applied at a scope. Global, product, API or operation. And every policy has four sections, which run in order."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("Inbound, before the request reaches the back end. Backend, the call to the back end itself. Outbound, after the back end replies. And on-error, when something in the pipeline throws."),
            leo("What if I set a policy on one operation? Do the wider ones still run?"),
            maya("Only if you keep the base element. It's where the parent scope's policies execute. Leave it out of an operation's inbound section, and the product and global inbound policies simply don't run. [pause] That's a favourite mistake."),
            leo("[sigh] I can see exactly how that happens."),
            maya("Now the pair that everyone mixes up. Rate limit, and quota."),
            leo("Aren't they the same thing?"),
            maya("No, and the status codes are different on purpose. Rate limit smooths bursts over a short window, say sixty calls a minute. Go over it and you get 429, Too Many Requests, which means slow down. A quota is a long-term allowance, say ten thousand calls a day, or a thousand a month. Go over it and you get 403, Forbidden, which means you've had your share."),
            leo("So partners allowed a thousand calls a month, where bursts are fine..."),
            maya("That's a quota, keyed by subscription. And ten calls a minute, telling them to slow down rather than refusing them, is a rate limit. Both come in by-key versions, so you can count per IP, per subscription, or by any expression."),
            leo("[laugh] Slow down, versus go away."),
            maya("That's a good way to remember it. Then there's a long list of other policies. Validate JWT checks a token, say an Entra ID token with a given audience. IP filter allows or denies ranges. Set header adds, changes or removes headers. Rewrite URI changes the back-end path, and set backend service routes to a different back end, for a canary."),
            leo("Any others worth knowing?"),
            maya("Cache lookup and cache store for response caching. Mock response, to return a fake response before the back end exists. CORS headers for browser callers. Converting between XML and JSON. Return response, to short-circuit with a maintenance message. And retry, for transient back-end errors."),
            leo("And if a policy needs a bit of logic?"),
            maya("Policy expressions. Small pieces of C sharp, inside the policy, with a context object that gives you the request, the response, the subscription, the user and variables."),
            leo("You mentioned securing the back end separately."),
            maya("Right. API Management can authenticate to the back end with a client certificate, a managed identity token, or Basic credentials. And the back end should only accept API Management, with an IP restriction, a private network, or by requiring the certificate. Otherwise callers just go around the front door."),
            leo("Is there an example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks exposes a logs API to a partner tutoring service. API Management validates the partner's Entra token, enforces sixty calls a minute per subscription, caches one read for five minutes, and strips internal headers on the way out. The back end only accepts traffic from the gateway, with a managed identity token, so nobody can skip it."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("APIs, operations, products and subscriptions, with the gateway running policies in four sections. Rate limit for bursts with 429, quota for allowances with 403, and keep the base element so parent policies still run. And lock the back end so only the gateway can reach it, Consumption for serverless, Premium for networks and regions."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("The Consumption tier is free for the first million calls a month. Create one, and import the conference sample API. Add a rate limit of three calls per sixty seconds. Then call it four times from the test console, and read the 429 and the trace."),
            leo("Getting told to slow down, on purpose. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val eventGrid = Podcast(
        "az-eventgrid",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Event Grid. Reacting to things that happened. An event just says \"this happened\". A blob was created. A resource was deleted. A user finished a lesson."),
            leo("And Event Grid does what with it?"),
            maya("It routes events from sources to handlers, with filters and retries, and with no polling. It's push, it's near real time, and you pay per million operations."),
            leo("Who are the players?"),
            maya("An event source is where it came from: Storage, a resource group, IoT Hub, Key Vault, or your own app. The source publishes to a topic, which is the endpoint events are sent to. A system topic is one Azure creates for an Azure service. A custom topic is one your app publishes to."),
            leo("And then?"),
            maya("An event subscription is a filtered route from a topic to one handler. And the handler is the destination. A function, a Logic App, a webhook, a Service Bus queue, Event Hubs, a storage queue."),
            leo("[chuckle] Lots of destinations. What's inside an event?"),
            maya("What happened, where, and when. An id, the topic, a subject, an event type like BlobCreated, a time, and a small data payload. Sixty-four kilobytes is free, up to one megabyte."),
            leo("You said filters. How fine-grained?"),
            maya("Three ways. By event type. By subject, with a begins-with prefix or an ends-with suffix. Or advanced filters on the data fields themselves, like a number greater than something, up to twenty-five conditions."),
            leo("So, only CSV files uploaded to the exports container..."),
            maya("Is a subject filter. Subject begins with the path to the exports container, and ends with dot CSV. [pause] And notice why you filter on the subscription, rather than checking at the top of your handler. A filtered-out event is never delivered at all, so it's never paid for or handled."),
            leo("Here's my real question. How many times does an event arrive?"),
            maya("At least once. So possibly more."),
            leo("[sigh] I was hoping you'd say exactly once."),
            maya("[laugh] Everyone hopes that. Delivery is at least once, with retries over twenty-four hours using exponential backoff. You can set the maximum attempts and a time to live. And that decides how your handler has to be written. It must be idempotent. The same event twice must have the same effect as once."),
            leo("How do I spot a repeat?"),
            maya("By the event id. Don't reject the duplicate, and don't turn retries off. Just make handling it twice harmless."),
            leo("And events that never get through?"),
            maya("Dead-letter them. After the retries run out, undeliverable events can be kept as blobs in a storage container, for you to inspect later."),
            leo("What about webhooks? Can anyone point Event Grid at any URL?"),
            maya("No, there's a validation handshake first. Before Event Grid trusts a webhook, it sends a subscription validation event with a validation code. Your endpoint must echo the code back in its response. Or, for a manual handshake, you visit the validation URL within five minutes."),
            leo("And if I'm using a function?"),
            maya("A function with an Event Grid trigger handles the handshake for you. But if you write your own webhook and return success before answering the handshake, the subscription stays unvalidated."),
            leo("Can my own app publish events?"),
            maya("Yes, to a custom topic. Your app sends an event with a subject, a type of its own, like LessonFinished, and its data. You can use the Event Grid schema, or CloudEvents one point oh, chosen per topic and per subscription."),
            leo("This sounds a lot like Event Hubs and Service Bus. How do I tell them apart?"),
            maya("That's the exam's favourite confusion, and the next topics drill it. Event Grid is for discrete events you react to. Event Hubs is for streams of telemetry at scale. And Service Bus is for commands and messages that must be processed. Using Event Grid for high-volume telemetry is one of the mistakes."),
            leo("Is there an example of it all working together?"),
            maya("The topic's one. When the nightly export lands in the exports container, Blob Storage raises BlobCreated. A subscription filtered to CSV files pushes it to the import function, which parses the file. A second subscription sends every event to a Service Bus queue for the audit service. And a corrupted file that makes the function throw is retried, then dead-lettered after twenty-four hours."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Sources publish to system or custom topics, and subscriptions filter and push to handlers. Filter by type, subject or data, with at-least-once delivery, twenty-four hours of retries and dead-lettering. And webhooks must answer the validation handshake, while every handler should be idempotent."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("The first hundred thousand operations a month are free. Create a storage account and an Event Grid trigger function. Add a system topic subscription for BlobCreated. Upload a file and read the event in the function log. Then add a subject suffix filter for PNG files, upload a text file, and see it skipped."),
            leo("Skipped on purpose. Very satisfying. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val eventHubs = Podcast(
        "az-eventhubs",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Event Hubs. Ingesting streams at scale. It takes in millions of small events a second, from many producers, and keeps them for a retention period. Consumers then read them, in order per partition, at their own pace, and more than once."),
            leo("More than once? That's not how a queue works."),
            maya("Exactly, and that's the key difference. Reading doesn't remove events. Many readers can read the same stream, and a reader can rewind. It's Azure's equivalent of Kafka, and it even speaks the Kafka protocol."),
            leo("Walk me through the pieces."),
            maya("A namespace is the container for your hubs, with its address and its tier. An event hub is one stream, like a Kafka topic. And a hub is split into partitions. A partition is an ordered log, and it's the unit of parallelism."),
            leo("Ordered across the whole hub?"),
            maya("No, and that's a trap. Order holds only inside a partition, never across them. So if one device's events must stay in order, you send them with a consistent partition key. Events with the same key are hashed to the same partition."),
            leo("Okay. Now the warm-up question. Two separate apps both need every event. What does each one need?"),
            maya("A consumer group each. A consumer group is an independent view of the whole stream, with its own position. Not a partition, which is only a slice. And not a second hub, which would be a copy."),
            leo("[chuckle] So partitions are slices, and consumer groups are viewers."),
            maya("That's it exactly. And if two apps share one consumer group, they steal each other's partitions. That's one of the mistakes."),
            leo("How does a reader remember where it was?"),
            maya("With a checkpoint. It's a saved position in a partition, stored in blob storage, so a reader can resume after a restart. In code, the EventProcessorClient spreads partitions across your running instances, and keeps ownership and checkpoints in a blob container."),
            leo("Should I checkpoint after every event?"),
            maya("After a batch. Every checkpoint is a storage write, so checkpointing on every event means paying for a write each time. [pause] And if you'd rather not write the client at all, the Functions Event Hub trigger does all of it for you, with a storage connection."),
            leo("How do I make it handle more?"),
            maya("Two levers. Throughput, and partitions. On Standard, a throughput unit gives you one megabyte a second in and two out. Turn on auto-inflate and the units grow under load. But it never shrinks back."),
            leo("[sigh] Of course it doesn't."),
            maya("[laugh] Then partitions. More partitions mean more parallel readers, with at most one reader per partition in each consumer group. And on Standard, the partition count is fixed once you create the hub."),
            leo("How long are events kept?"),
            maya("One to seven days on Standard, up to ninety on Premium. And remember, they're not deleted when they're read. So to replay yesterday's events, you use retention, plus a new consumer group starting from an earlier position."),
            leo("What if I need every event in storage, permanently?"),
            maya("Turn on Capture. It writes events in batches to Blob Storage or a data lake, as Avro files, with no code at all. You set a time window, one to fifteen minutes, or a size window. \"Archive every event to storage with no code\" is always Capture."),
            leo("Now the confusing part. How is this different from Event Grid and Service Bus?"),
            maya("Event Hubs is for streams. Telemetry, logs, clickstreams. Millions a second, retained, replayable, ordered per partition, and consumers pull. Event Grid is for discrete events, something happened, pushed to handlers. And Service Bus is for messages and commands that must each be processed."),
            leo("So if each message needs individual handling, with dead-lettering..."),
            maya("That's Service Bus, not Event Hubs. Choosing Event Hubs there is a mistake."),
            leo("Example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Both partners' phones send a heartbeat every minute while a study timer runs. Low value individually, high volume. With the owner as the partition key, each person's stream stays ordered. One consumer group feeds a live studying-now badge. Another aggregates minutes per day for reports. And Capture archives everything to the data lake."),
            leo("And if a third service needs to read it too?"),
            maya("Give it its own consumer group, with its checkpoints in its own container, so it can be reset without touching the others. Readers are separated by consumer group, never by partition."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Event Hubs ingests streams, and events are retained and replayable, ordered per partition. Consumer groups give each app its own view, and checkpoints in blob storage resume the reading. And you scale with throughput units and partitions, while Capture archives with no code."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("A Basic namespace costs about a cent an hour. Create one, with a hub of two partitions. Send ten events from the command line, or a small console app. Read them with the Functions Event Hub trigger, or the processor sample. Then check the hub's metrics for incoming and outgoing messages."),
            leo("Ten little events, read twice if I like. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val serviceBus = Podcast(
        "az-servicebus",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, messages that must be handled. Service Bus, and Queue Storage. A message is a command, or a piece of work. Send this email. Generate this certificate. It must be processed, usually once, and the sender wants to carry on without waiting."),
            leo("And Azure has two ways to do that."),
            maya("Two queue services. Service Bus, for enterprise features. And Queue Storage, for huge, simple queues. Let's start with Service Bus."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("A namespace holds everything, in a tier: Basic, Standard or Premium. Inside, a queue is a store where one receiver takes each message, so several workers compete for them. And a topic is publish once, with each subscription getting its own copy."),
            leo("Topics need a particular tier?"),
            maya("Standard or above. Premium adds dedicated capacity, and much larger messages. [pause] Now, the bit the exam loves. How a receiver takes a message. There are two receive modes. PeekLock, which is the default, and ReceiveAndDelete."),
            leo("What's the difference?"),
            maya("In PeekLock, the message is locked rather than removed. You receive it, process it, and then settle it. If you crash, the lock expires, and the message reappears for someone else. ReceiveAndDelete is faster, but if you fail, the message is gone."),
            leo("So PeekLock for anything that can fail."),
            maya("Always. And there are four ways to settle a locked message. Complete removes it, the work is done. Abandon releases the lock, so it's delivered again, with the delivery count going up by one. Dead-letter moves it to the dead-letter queue, with a reason. And defer keeps it, but skips it, so you fetch it later by its sequence number."),
            leo("And the warm-up question. A message I can never process, no matter what."),
            maya("Dead-letter it. Completing it would claim the work succeeded and lose it quietly. Abandoning it would just bring it back, again and again, until the delivery count ran out."),
            leo("[chuckle] Which it would, eventually."),
            maya("Ten tries by default, and then it's dead-lettered anyway. Every queue and every subscription has its own dead-letter queue. [pause] And read it. Never reading the dead-letter queue means failures pile up silently."),
            leo("What if the work takes a long time?"),
            maya("The lock lasts thirty seconds by default, up to five minutes, and you can renew it. If you process for longer than the lock without renewing, the message is redelivered to another worker while you're still on it."),
            leo("[sigh] Two workers doing the same job. What about ordering?"),
            maya("That's sessions. You group messages by a session id, on a session-enabled queue, and each session gets strict order, with one receiver at a time. So messages for one order, or one person, are handled in sequence."),
            leo("And the other features?"),
            maya("Duplicate detection drops repeated message ids within a window, up to seven days. Scheduled messages are delivered at a time you set. Auto-forwarding chains one entity into another. And transactions group operations, so you can complete one message and send another, atomically."),
            leo("How do topic subscriptions choose what they get?"),
            maya("With rules. A SQL filter, which is flexible. A correlation filter, which matches properties exactly, and is cheaper. Or a boolean filter, true or false. And here's a mistake to avoid. A new subscription starts with a default rule that matches everything. Leave it in place beside your own rule, and every message still matches."),
            leo("Okay. Now Queue Storage. Why would I pick it?"),
            maya("Size, simplicity and cost. A Service Bus message is up to two hundred and fifty-six kilobytes, or a hundred megabytes on Premium, and a queue tops out at eighty gigabytes. A Storage queue message is up to sixty-four kilobytes, but the queue can grow to five hundred terabytes."),
            leo("So, a queue that must exceed eighty gigabytes..."),
            maya("Is Storage queues. So is a server-side audit log of every operation. But duplicate detection, a first-in first-out guarantee, transactions, or publish and subscribe all point to Service Bus."),
            leo("How does a Storage queue work, then? No PeekLock?"),
            maya("No, it has a visibility timeout instead. You receive a message and it's hidden for that long. You process it, then delete it, using the pop receipt, which is the token proving you hold it. If you don't delete it in time, it becomes visible again."),
            leo("Example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. When a quiz is passed, the API publishes a QuizPassed message to a lesson-events topic. The certificates subscription, with a correlation filter, generates a PDF, and analytics takes everything."),
            leo("And the worker that makes the PDF?"),
            maya("It uses PeekLock. It completes on success, abandons on a passing storage error, and dead-letters a malformed message. Profile photo thumbnails, though, go through a Storage queue, because the work is trivial and the volume is high."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Service Bus queues for one-to-one work, and topics with filtered subscriptions for fan-out. PeekLock, then complete, abandon, dead-letter or defer, with sessions for ordering, and duplicate detection and scheduling built in. And Storage queues for huge, simple queues, with a visibility timeout and the pop receipt."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Create a Standard namespace, with a topic and two subscriptions. Add a SQL filter rule to one of them, and delete its default rule. Send two messages with different properties from Service Bus Explorer in the portal. Then peek each subscription, and see which one got what."),
            leo("Two messages, two mailboxes. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val appInsights = Podcast(
        "az-appinsights",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Application Insights. Seeing what your code actually does in production. It's the part of Azure Monitor that watches your application. Every request, every dependency call, every exception, plus anything you choose to log."),
            leo("And where does all that go?"),
            maya("Into a Log Analytics workspace, which you query with KQL, the Kusto Query Language. Each kind of telemetry has its own table. Requests, dependencies, exceptions, traces, custom events, custom metrics, page views from the JavaScript SDK, and availability results."),
            leo("Do I have to write code to get any of it?"),
            maya("Not for the basics. Auto-instrumentation works on App Service, Functions and AKS with no code change. You toggle it in the portal, and you get requests, dependencies and exceptions. What you don't get is custom events."),
            leo("And for those?"),
            maya("The SDK, now the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry distribution. You add a package and the connection string, and then you can track events and metrics yourself, with your own properties, and configure sampling."),
            leo("Connection string, or instrumentation key? I've seen both."),
            maya("Connection string. The old instrumentation key on its own is the past, and new regions require the connection string. [pause] Now, custom events. They take two kinds of extra data. String properties, for filtering. And numeric measurements, for charts."),
            leo("So, counting how many lessons were finished, split by topic..."),
            maya("Is a custom event, with the topic as a property. Not a trace, and not an exception. Custom events with properties are made for counting business actions and splitting them by a dimension."),
            leo("[chuckle] Business actions. Okay. And the KQL bit?"),
            maya("A handful of operators carry you a long way. Where filters rows. Summarize, by something, groups and aggregates, count, average, percentile. Extend adds a column, project picks columns. Join combines tables on a key. And render charts the result."),
            leo("Here's the warm-up question. One request makes four database calls and throws. What ties those six items together?"),
            maya("A shared operation id. Every request, and every dependency it caused, carries the same operation id. Not the timestamp, and not the app's role name. The Application Map and the end-to-end transaction view are both built on it."),
            leo("So following a slow request through three services..."),
            maya("Is the operation id and the end-to-end view. [pause] Now, cost. Telemetry can get expensive under heavy load, which is what sampling is for."),
            leo("Doesn't sampling make my numbers wrong?"),
            maya("That's the clever part. Adaptive sampling, the default in the dot net SDK, drops a fraction of items under load, but it keeps related items together, and it corrects the counts. So your request counts stay roughly right while the volume drops."),
            leo("[sigh] I'd assumed the counts would just fall."),
            maya("[laugh] A lot of people do, and the exam knows it. There's also fixed-rate sampling, which keeps a set percentage, and ingestion sampling, which happens in the portal after the data arrives."),
            leo("And watching a deployment as it happens?"),
            maya("Live Metrics. It shows requests, failures and CPU with about one second of latency, while you deploy. So, exam phrases. \"Reduce ingestion cost while keeping counts accurate\" is sampling. \"Follow a slow request through three services\" is the operation id. \"Watch a deployment live\" is Live Metrics."),
            leo("Is there a real example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. The Two Tracks API saw a spike in server errors one evening. In Failures, the top exception was a Cosmos 429 inside the logs endpoint. The end-to-end view showed the dependency call taking two seconds before the retry gave up."),
            leo("And the cause?"),
            maya("A KQL summarize by hour lined it up with the nightly export job. The fix was autoscale on the container, plus a custom event on each export, so the two can be correlated next time."),
            leo("Mistakes to watch for?"),
            maya("Logging personal data in custom properties, because telemetry is kept for ninety days by default and anyone with reader access can query it. Turning off sampling under heavy load and paying for every item. Using the instrumentation key alone. And looking in the wrong table, because exceptions you log through ILogger land in traces, unless you pass the exception object itself."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Requests, dependencies, exceptions and traces come free, and custom events and metrics add your own. Everything lands in a Log Analytics workspace, queried with KQL. And the operation id correlates, sampling cuts cost, and Live Metrics watches deployments."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("There's a five gigabyte free monthly allowance. Create an Application Insights resource. Run any sample web app with its connection string. Make a few requests, and one that fails. Then, in Logs, summarize the requests by their result code."),
            leo("A query of my very own. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val availability = Podcast(
        "az-availability",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, a short one with a simple idea behind it. Monitoring is only useful if somebody is told. So, availability tests, alerts, and autoscale rules."),
            leo("Start with availability tests."),
            maya("They probe your site from the outside. The standard test sends an HTTP request from up to sixteen regions, and checks the status code, the content, and that the TLS certificate is valid. It's the default for any public endpoint."),
            leo("What if my endpoint isn't public?"),
            maya("Then a standard test can't reach it, because it runs from Microsoft's public regions. Instead, you run your own probe inside the private network, often a function on a timer, and it reports the result with TrackAvailability."),
            leo("[chuckle] So the inside reports out."),
            maya("Exactly. Standard tests probe from outside. Anything private has to report in from inside. And the old URL ping test is retired, so migrate those to standard tests."),
            leo("Okay. Now alerts."),
            maya("An alert rule turns a condition into a notification. There are a few kinds. A metric alert compares a metric against a threshold, like server response time above two seconds for five minutes. A log search alert runs a KQL query on a schedule, like more than ten exceptions of one type in fifteen minutes."),
            leo("And the others?"),
            maya("An activity log alert watches management operations, so someone deleting a resource. Smart detection spots anomalies on its own, like a failure rate that rises unusually. And an availability alert fires when enough test locations fail in a window, say three of five regions."),
            leo("So if the question says \"run a query on a schedule\"..."),
            maya("That's a log search alert, and only a log search alert. [pause] A few details. Severity runs from zero, critical, to four, verbose. A dynamic threshold learns the normal range, instead of you picking a fixed number. And an alert has a state, fired, acknowledged or closed, with auto-resolve closing metric alerts when the condition clears."),
            leo("Who actually gets told?"),
            maya("Whoever's in the action group. It's a reusable set of notification and automation actions, an email, a push to the Azure mobile app, and so on. Sending alerts to one person's inbox is a mistake, because the list can't change without editing every rule."),
            leo("And during maintenance? I don't want my phone buzzing all night."),
            maya("Alert processing rules can suppress notifications during a maintenance window, or add action groups at scale."),
            leo("[laugh] Good. Now autoscale. Didn't we do that with App Service?"),
            maya("We did, and here's the bigger picture. Autoscale lives in Azure Monitor, and it applies to App Service plans, virtual machine scale sets and more. A profile has a minimum, a maximum and a default instance count, plus rules that scale out and in."),
            leo("What kinds of rules?"),
            maya("A scale out rule, like CPU above seventy percent for ten minutes, add one, with a five-minute cooldown. A scale in rule, like CPU below thirty percent, remove one. A schedule rule, weekdays eight till six, keep at least three. And a queue-based rule, using a metric from another resource, like more than five hundred Service Bus messages, add two."),
            leo("Here's the warm-up. Add one above seventy, remove one below seventy."),
            maya("It flaps. The two rules meet at the same number, so they argue. Add, remove, add, remove. The fix is a gap between the thresholds, out at seventy and in at thirty, plus a cooldown, which is the pause after a scale action before the next evaluation."),
            leo("[sigh] And if I only have a scale out rule?"),
            maya("The plan never shrinks, and the bill keeps growing. Always pair it with a scale in rule. [pause] And remember what autoscale does. It changes the number of instances. Scaling up, to a bigger tier, is a manual change."),
            leo("Is there an example of all three together?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks has a standard availability test from five regions every five minutes, with an alert when three fail. A metric alert on more than five server errors in five minutes pages Usaid through the action group, with a push and an email. And autoscale keeps one instance at night, adding up to three when CPU passes seventy percent, with a ten-minute cooldown."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Standard tests probe public endpoints, and TrackAvailability covers private ones. Metric, log search, activity log and availability alerts all send to action groups. And autoscale pairs scale out and scale in rules, with a cooldown and a gap between the thresholds."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Alerts cost cents, and a few availability tests are free. Add a standard availability test to any public URL. Create an action group with your email. Create a metric alert on the availability results dropping below a hundred percent. Then stop the app, and wait for the email to arrive."),
            leo("Breaking my own app, on purpose, to get an email. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val redis = Podcast(
        "az-redis",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Azure Cache for Redis. Fast reads, and the patterns around them. Redis is an in-memory key-value store, and reads take under a millisecond."),
            leo("So what belongs in it?"),
            maya("Anything you compute often and change rarely. A user's plan for today. A session. A leaderboard. A rate-limit counter. [pause] But first, a question. How many Redis connection objects should a web app create?"),
            leo("One per request? That sounds tidy."),
            maya("[chuckle] That's the classic mistake. One, shared for the life of the app. The connection object, the ConnectionMultiplexer in the dot net client, is expensive to build and designed to be shared. Creating one per request exhausts the connections."),
            leo("So register it once, as a singleton."),
            maya("Exactly. Now, tiers. Basic is a single node with no SLA, so dev and test only. Standard is two replicated nodes, with a ninety-nine point nine percent SLA, for production without large scale. Premium adds clustering, persistence, network injection, geo-replication and bigger sizes."),
            leo("And above Premium?"),
            maya("Enterprise, which adds the Redis Enterprise modules, like RediSearch, RedisJSON and time series, and active geo-replication. And Enterprise Flash, which puts very large caches on fast flash storage, for less."),
            leo("Exam phrases?"),
            maya("\"Must persist to disk\" or \"inside a private network\" means Premium at least. \"RediSearch\" or \"active-active across regions\" means Enterprise. And Basic in production is a mistake, with no replica and no SLA."),
            leo("What can I actually store?"),
            maya("A few structures, each fitting a pattern. A string, with an expiry, caches a value. Incrementing a string counts things, for rate limiting. A hash is an object with fields. A list makes a simple queue. And a sorted set keeps members ordered by a score."),
            leo("Which is a leaderboard."),
            maya("Exactly. A sorted set gives you the top few quickly. Not a string per user, and not a list. [pause] Now, the patterns. The default is cache-aside. The app reads the cache first. On a miss, it reads the real source, and puts the value in the cache with a time to live, a TTL."),
            leo("And when the data changes?"),
            maya("You invalidate on write. Delete the key, so the next read rebuilds it from the source. Waiting for the TTL instead would serve stale data until it expired. The TTL is the safety net, not the plan."),
            leo("[laugh] The safety net, not the plan. I like that."),
            maya("There's also write-through, where writes go to the cache and the database together, so the cache is always current, at the cost of extra write latency. And the session store, where user sessions live in Redis instead of the web server, so you can scale out freely."),
            leo("What happens when the cache fills up?"),
            maya("The eviction policy decides which keys go. The default evicts the least recently used keys that have an expiry first. Which is why keys without an expiry are a mistake, memory fills, and eviction picks for you. You can also reserve some memory as headroom."),
            leo("Can I trust Redis to keep my data?"),
            maya("No, and that's important. Redis is not a database. Assume anything in it can disappear, and always keep the slow path, the one that reads from the real store."),
            leo("[sigh] Good to know before I learn it the hard way."),
            maya("[chuckle] And on security, use managed identity, with Entra ID authentication, instead of access keys where it's supported. Or keep the connection string in Key Vault. One more mistake to avoid is caching one user's data under a shared key."),
            leo("Example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Building Usaid's Today page costs about thirty Cosmos request units and two hundred milliseconds of planning. With cache-aside and a ten-minute TTL, opening it again costs one millisecond. When he logs a session, the API deletes his plan key for that date, so the next read rebuilds it."),
            leo("And the streaks?"),
            maya("The streak leaderboard for both partners is a sorted set, updated by a change feed function."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Redis is in memory and fast, with Standard for production, Premium for private networks and persistence, and Enterprise for modules. Use cache-aside with a TTL, invalidate on write, and one multiplexer per app. And strings, hashes, sorted sets and lists each fit a pattern: values, objects, leaderboards and queues."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("A Basic C0 cache costs a few cents an hour. Create one, and open the portal console. Set a greeting with a sixty-second expiry, then get it back. Wait a minute, and get it again. Then add two streaks to a sorted set, and read them back highest first, with their scores."),
            leo("A greeting that disappears. Very dramatic. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val cdn = Podcast(
        "az-cdn",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, caching at the edge. Azure Front Door, and the CDN. A content delivery network keeps copies of your static files on servers close to your users."),
            leo("How close?"),
            maya("Close enough that the first visitor in Doha fetches a file from your origin, and everyone after them in the region gets the edge copy. The origin is where the CDN fetches from on a miss. An edge, or point of presence, is a server near the users that holds the cached copies."),
            leo("And Front Door is the CDN?"),
            maya("Front Door is the current product. Front Door Standard is a global entry point, with CDN, TLS, routing and basic web application firewall rules, and it replaces Azure CDN from Microsoft. Front Door Premium adds managed firewall rules, and Private Link to your origins, for security-sensitive apps."),
            leo("And the classic CDN profiles?"),
            maya("Still in exam questions. Same caching ideas, older product. [pause] So, the big question. What decides how long an edge keeps its copy?"),
            leo("The tier, maybe? Or the file size?"),
            maya("[chuckle] Neither. The origin's Cache-Control header sets the time to live, the TTL, unless a caching rule overrides it. If the header's missing, Front Door uses a default, a day or a few days depending on the product."),
            leo("What kinds of caching rule are there?"),
            maya("Three. Honor origin, which obeys the origin's Cache-Control, and is the default. Override, which sets your own TTL regardless, for static assets that arrive with no headers. And bypass cache, which never caches, for personalised or dynamic responses."),
            leo("What about query strings? Is each URL its own copy?"),
            maya("You choose. Ignore query strings, and there's one cached copy. Use them, and there's a copy per unique URL. Or include or exclude specific parameters. Choosing \"use query string\" for parameters you never actually change just wastes cache, one copy per URL."),
            leo("Now the warm-up. A file is cached everywhere, and it has to change for everyone right now."),
            maya("Two options. A purge removes cached content before its TTL, by path or by wildcard. It works, and it's the tool for an emergency. But it takes a few minutes, it's per path, and it's a request to every edge."),
            leo("And the more dependable one?"),
            maya("Change the file name, so the URL is new. A versioned file name, with a content hash in it, can't be served stale, because no edge holds a copy of a URL it has never seen. So versioned names avoid purges altogether."),
            leo("[laugh] Can't be stale if it's brand new. Fair."),
            maya("And dropping the TTL to one second isn't the answer either. That just throws away the point of the CDN. [pause] Edges can also compress text files on the way out, if you enable compression."),
            leo("What goes wrong in practice?"),
            maya("The worst one is caching API responses by accident, and serving one user's data to another. Personal responses need bypass cache. Then there's forgetting that a purge is slow and per path. And pointing the origin at the blob endpoint, instead of the static website endpoint."),
            leo("[sigh] Serving someone else's data. That's the nightmare."),
            maya("It is. So, exam phrases. \"A file was updated, but users still see the old version\" is a purge, or a versioned file name. \"Different users need different content under the same URL\" means bypass the cache, or vary on a query string."),
            leo("Example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks serves its help pages and lesson diagrams from a static website in blob storage, behind Front Door. The diagrams are named with a content hash, so a new version has a new URL, and no purge is needed."),
            leo("And the help pages themselves?"),
            maya("The help page has a Cache-Control max age of five minutes, so edits show within five minutes. And the API is routed with caching bypassed."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Front Door is the current CDN and global entry point, and the classic CDN profiles share the same ideas. Cache-Control from the origin, or an override rule, sets the TTL, and query string behaviour decides how many copies there are. And purge by path for urgent changes, while versioned file names avoid the need."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Front Door Standard has an hourly fee, so delete it afterwards. Enable a static website on a storage account. Create a Front Door Standard profile pointing at it. Load the page twice and check the cache header, a miss and then a hit. Then change the file, see the old one, purge it, and see the new one."),
            leo("A miss, then a hit. Like my first attempts at the exam. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val identity = Podcast(
        "az-identity",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, the Microsoft identity platform. App registrations, tokens and permissions. It's the sign-in service behind Microsoft accounts and Entra ID, which is the new name for Azure Active Directory."),
            leo("And the basic idea?"),
            maya("You register your app once, and it gets an id. From then on, users sign in with Microsoft, and your app receives tokens that prove who they are and what they may do."),
            leo("Tokens, plural?"),
            maya("Three kinds, with different jobs. An ID token proves who the user is, and it's for your client app, with claims like name and email. An access token proves what the caller may do on an API, and it's sent to the API, in the authorization header. And a refresh token lets the client get new tokens silently."),
            leo("Can my client read the access token to decide what to show?"),
            maya("No, and that's one of the mistakes. Access tokens are for the API, never for your client's own decisions. Read the ID token for the user's name. Send the access token to the API. And on the API side, validate the audience claim, because a token for Microsoft Graph is not a token for you."),
            leo("[chuckle] Right, someone else's ticket. What does registering create?"),
            maya("An application object, the single registration, in your home tenant. And in every tenant that uses the app, a service principal. That's the per-tenant identity that actually gets permissions and roles. One application object, a service principal per tenant."),
            leo("What goes into a registration?"),
            maya("The supported account types, which is who can sign in. A redirect URI, which is where tokens are returned. A client secret or certificate, which proves the app's own identity, for confidential clients only, and a certificate is preferred. Expose an API, which lists the scopes your API offers. And API permissions, which are the scopes this app requests from other APIs."),
            leo("Okay. Now the warm-up. A nightly job, nobody signed in, reading everyone's calendar."),
            maya("That needs an application permission, consented by an admin. There are two kinds of permission, and the deciding question is whether a person is there."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("A delegated permission is a signed-in user acting through the app. The user consents, or an admin consents for everyone, and it shows up in the token as the scope claim. An application permission is the app acting as itself, with no user at all. An admin always consents, and it shows up as the roles claim."),
            leo("So delegated for a user reading their own files..."),
            maya("And application for a job with no user. The exam tests that split constantly. [pause] And notice why delegated can't work for the nightly job. With no user, there's nobody for it to act on behalf of. Requesting application permissions for a user-facing flow is the opposite mistake."),
            leo("What are public and confidential clients?"),
            maya("A public client is a mobile app, a desktop app, or a single-page app. It can't keep a secret, so it uses the authorization code flow with PKCE, and its redirect is a custom scheme or localhost. A confidential client is a web app or a daemon on a server. It holds a secret or certificate, and it can request application permissions."),
            leo("[sigh] So no secrets in mobile apps."),
            maya("Never. Putting a client secret in a mobile app is a mistake. Public clients use PKCE, Proof Key for Code Exchange, which protects the authorization code instead."),
            leo("Which flows do I need to recognise?"),
            maya("Five. Authorization code, with PKCE, when a user signs in through a browser. It's the default for web, mobile and single-page apps. Client credentials, for a daemon or a job with no user, using application permissions and a secret or certificate."),
            leo("And the other three?"),
            maya("On-behalf-of, when an API calls another API as the user, exchanging the incoming token for a new one. Device code, when there's no browser on the device, like a command line tool or a TV, and the user enters a code somewhere else. And implicit, which is legacy and deprecated. Choose authorization code with PKCE instead."),
            leo("[laugh] Five flows. I'll be dreaming of redirects. Is there an example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks registers two apps. The mobile client, which is public, and the API, which exposes a Logs read-write scope. The client requests that scope, plus Graph's User Read."),
            leo("And when Usaid signs in?"),
            maya("He consents once. The client gets an ID token for his name, an access token for the API with the Logs scope, and another for Graph, for his profile photo. And a nightly report function uses client credentials, with an application permission consented by the admin."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("The ID token says who, the access token says what the API may be asked, and the API validates the audience. One application object, a service principal per tenant, and permissions attach to the principal. And delegated means a user is present, application means no user and admin consent, with authorization code and PKCE for users, and client credentials for daemons."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Any free Entra tenant works. Register an app with a localhost redirect URI. Note its client id and tenant id. Add Microsoft Graph's User Read under API permissions. Then open Authentication, and compare the public client and web platform settings."),
            leo("My very own service principal. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val msal = Podcast(
        "az-msal",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, MSAL. Acquiring tokens in code. MSAL, the Microsoft Authentication Library, is the library that talks to the identity platform for you. You tell it which app you are and which scopes you need, and it handles the browser, the token cache and the refreshing."),
            leo("So I never build token requests by hand."),
            maya("Never. And there are two builders, matching the two kinds of client. The public client application builder is for mobile, desktop and command line apps. The confidential client application builder is for web apps, APIs and daemons."),
            leo("And the methods on each?"),
            maya("On a public client, AcquireTokenSilent, AcquireTokenInteractive, and AcquireTokenWithDeviceCode. On a confidential client, AcquireTokenByAuthorizationCode, AcquireTokenForClient, and AcquireTokenOnBehalfOf."),
            leo("[chuckle] That's a lot of Acquire Token. Which one first?"),
            maya("Silent, always. AcquireTokenSilent returns a cached or refreshed token with no prompt, and that's the common case. [pause] Now the warm-up question. Silent throws an MsalUiRequiredException. What's it telling you?"),
            leo("Retry, in case the service is busy?"),
            maya("No. The name is doing the work. It means the user has to be asked something. Consent, multi-factor authentication, or a session that has expired. So you fall back to an interactive prompt. Retrying silently can't change what's in the cache, it'll just throw again."),
            leo("So the pattern is silent first, interactive on that exception."),
            maya("Exactly that. And the exam shows code that calls interactive on every request, and asks what's wrong. The answer is that users see prompts constantly."),
            leo("[laugh] That would drive me mad. What about a service with no user at all?"),
            maya("That's client credentials, so AcquireTokenForClient on a confidential client. And the scope it asks for is the resource with slash dot default on the end. Dot default means every permission already consented for that resource on the app registration."),
            leo("Why not just ask for User Read?"),
            maya("Because there's no user to consent to anything narrower. Client credentials has to use dot default. [pause] Scopes in general are written as the resource, then the permission. Graph's User Read, or your own API's Logs read-write."),
            leo("Can I ask for Graph scopes and my API's scopes in one go?"),
            maya("No, and it's a trap. One access token is for one resource. It has a single audience. So ask for Graph scopes and your API's scopes in separate calls. If a question mixes scopes from two APIs in one request and asks why it fails, that's why."),
            leo("And the other OpenID scopes I see? Openid, profile..."),
            maya("Those are OpenID Connect scopes. And offline access is the one that gets you a refresh token."),
            leo("Is there an example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. The Two Tracks Android app uses MSAL for Android. On launch it calls acquire token silent. On the very first run that throws, so it shows the Microsoft sign-in and caches the account. From then on, silent works for months."),
            leo("For months? Even when the access token expires?"),
            maya("Yes, because of the refresh token. A month later, the cached access token has long expired, but silent still succeeds without a prompt, because the refresh token fetches a new one. Only when that fails too does the exception come back, and the user is asked."),
            leo("[sigh] That's the bit I'd have got wrong. What about web apps?"),
            maya("Use the authorization code flow through Microsoft Identity Web, which wraps MSAL for ASP.NET Core. And keep the token cache in a distributed cache, Redis or SQL. In-memory caches vanish when you scale out."),
            leo("So caching tokens in memory on a web farm..."),
            maya("Is one of the mistakes. The others are calling interactive on every request, using a secret in a public client, and mixing Graph and your own API's scopes in one call."),
            leo("And an API that calls another API as the user?"),
            maya("That's AcquireTokenOnBehalfOf. And a command line tool on a machine with no browser uses AcquireTokenWithDeviceCode."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("The public client builder for apps with users, and the confidential builder for servers and daemons. Silent first, interactive only on MsalUiRequiredException, and AcquireTokenForClient for jobs with no user. And one resource per token, dot default for client credentials, and offline access for refresh tokens."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Use the app registration from the previous topic. Create a dot net console app, and add the MSAL package. Acquire a token for User Read interactively. Paste the access token into a token decoder, and read its audience and scope claims. Then run it again, and confirm the silent path succeeds."),
            leo("Silent success. My favourite kind. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val graph = Podcast(
        "az-graph",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Microsoft Graph. One API for Microsoft 365 data. It's a single REST endpoint for users, mail, calendars, files, Teams and more. And you call it with an access token whose audience is Graph."),
            leo("Not my own API's token, then."),
            maya("No, and sending your own API's token to Graph is the first mistake on the list. The audience must be Graph. [pause] Now, shaping a call. Graph uses OData query parameters, and they're worth knowing by name."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("Select returns only the properties you ask for, say the display name and mail. Filter is the where clause. Orderby sorts. Top and skip set the page size and the offset. Expand includes a related resource, like a group's members. Count includes a total. And search does full text."),
            leo("Any catch with those?"),
            maya("A favourite exam detail. Count, search, and some filters on directory objects need the header ConsistencyLevel: eventual. Forget it, and those queries don't work."),
            leo("[chuckle] One little header. Okay. What happens when there are lots of results?"),
            maya("They come in pages. A response that has more carries a next link, which is the complete URL of the following page. You call it, and keep following, until a response arrives without one."),
            leo("So if my call returns only the first hundred items..."),
            maya("Follow the next link. Not some magic parameter for everything, and not raising skip until the results are empty. Ignoring the next link and reporting partial results is another of the mistakes."),
            leo("Does select really make that much difference?"),
            maya("It does. In Graph Explorer, get yourself, and then get yourself again selecting only the display name, and watch how much smaller the response is. Ask only for the fields you need. And top asks for a bigger page, so you make fewer calls in the first place."),
            leo("So, shape the request first, and then page through."),
            maya("That's the order for getting a large list out without hundreds of calls. Shape it with select, filter and top. Follow the next link until there isn't one. And combine the independent calls into batches."),
            leo("And the warm-up. Thirty independent calls. Fewest requests?"),
            maya("Two. You can batch requests, by posting to the batch endpoint, and one batch carries up to twenty requests in a single HTTP call, each with its own id and its own status."),
            leo("[laugh] So thirty is two batches, not one."),
            maya("Exactly, and that detail is precisely what the exam asks. Twenty per batch."),
            leo("What if Graph decides I'm calling too much?"),
            maya("You get a 429, which means you've been throttled. Honour the Retry-After header and wait that long. The Graph SDKs retry for you."),
            leo("Is there an example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks shows a partner's profile photo, and their next free calendar slot for a joint study session. With delegated permissions, User Read and Calendars Read, it calls the photo endpoint and the get schedule endpoint."),
            leo("And that's a separate token from the app's own API?"),
            maya("A separate token, because one access token has one audience. Graph for the photo and calendar, and the Two Tracks API for everything else. In the API itself, the Graph SDK uses DefaultAzureCredential."),
            leo("What if I need three things about one user? Their profile, their photo and their manager."),
            maya("Three separate calls would work, and would be three round trips. Send them together in one batch instead."),
            leo("[sigh] How do I even find out which scope a call needs?"),
            maya("Graph Explorer. It needs no setup. You sign in with a test account, run a call, and the permissions tab shows which scope each call needs. Then you copy those into your app registration. It's the best way to explore before writing code."),
            leo("Anything else to avoid?"),
            maya("Using the beta endpoint in production. Use version one point oh. And forgetting the ConsistencyLevel: eventual header with count or search."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("One endpoint, version one point oh for production, and the token's audience must be Graph. Shape results with select, filter and top, follow the next link for more, and batch up to twenty. And advanced queries need ConsistencyLevel: eventual, while a 429 means wait for Retry-After."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Ten minutes in Graph Explorer. Get yourself, then your top three messages with only their subjects selected. Then list users with a count, adding the ConsistencyLevel header. And look at the permissions tab for each call."),
            leo("Ten minutes, three calls, one header. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val appConfig = Podcast(
        "az-appconfig",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, App Configuration. Central settings, and feature flags. It's a central store for settings that are not secrets. Feature flags, limits, URLs, colours."),
            leo("Why not just keep them in each app's settings?"),
            maya("Because then every app has its own copy. With App Configuration, all your apps read from one place. You change a value once, and every instance picks it up. And labels let the same key differ per environment."),
            leo("Labels?"),
            maya("A label is a variant of the same key, usually per environment. Prod, dev, or none at all. In the topic's example, the dev label sets the daily limit to fifty for testing, while prod keeps fifteen."),
            leo("What else is in the store besides keys and values?"),
            maya("A content type says how to read a value, say JSON for something structured. A Key Vault reference is a pointer to a secret, which the app resolves with its own identity. A feature flag is a special key with optional filters. And a snapshot is a frozen, named set of keys, like everything as it was for one release."),
            leo("So secrets don't live in App Configuration."),
            maya("Never as plain values. That's the first mistake on the list. Store a Key Vault reference instead, and keep the secret in the vault. [pause] Now the warm-up. Settings change, and the app must pick them up without restarting. How many keys does it watch?"),
            leo("All of them, surely?"),
            maya("[chuckle] One. A sentinel key. The app watches a single sentinel key, and when that one changes, it refreshes every other key. That's one request instead of hundreds."),
            leo("And who does the checking? A timer?"),
            maya("The middleware. The refresh happens on the next request after the refresh interval, not on a background timer. Which is why forgetting to add the App Configuration middleware is another mistake. Without it, the refresh never triggers."),
            leo("So registering every single key for refresh..."),
            maya("Is exactly what the sentinel pattern exists to avoid. Register the sentinel, refresh everything with it. [pause] And the app code doesn't change at all. It reads the values as it always did."),
            leo("[laugh] Lazy code. My favourite kind. Now feature flags."),
            maya("A flag can be on, off, or conditional, with feature filters deciding its answer at runtime. A percentage filter turns it on for a stable share of users. A time window filter turns it on between two times. A targeting filter picks particular users and groups. And you can write custom filters."),
            leo("So showing a new feature to ten percent of users first..."),
            maya("Is a percentage filter. Not a time window, and not targeting a group. And a feature that should be on for a fifth of requests is a percentage filter too, not a label, and not a sentinel key."),
            leo("How does code check a flag?"),
            maya("Through the feature manager. You ask whether a flag is enabled, anywhere in your C sharp. In a Razor view there's a feature tag helper. And on a controller or an action there's a feature gate attribute. Variants go further, and let a flag return a value, not just on or off, which is handy for A B tests."),
            leo("How does the app get in? With a connection string?"),
            maya("Better with its managed identity, granted the App Configuration Data Reader role. Using connection strings when the app already has a managed identity is one more of the mistakes."),
            leo("[sigh] Identity again. It really is everywhere."),
            maya("It really is. The topic's example pulls it together. Two Tracks moved the daily limit and the mock exam flag into App Configuration. Turning the mock exam on for the beta group lets Sanya try it first. The API reads the store with its managed identity, and the Cosmos connection is a Key Vault reference inside the store."),
            leo("Is there a free tier?"),
            maya("Yes. One store per subscription, with a thousand requests a day. Standard is for production."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Central, non-secret settings, with labels for environments and Key Vault references for secrets. Dynamic refresh with a sentinel key and the middleware, and identity-based access with Data Reader. And feature flags, with percentage, time window and targeting filters, read through the feature manager."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("The free tier is enough. Create a free store, with two labelled values for one key. Add a feature flag, and toggle it. In a console app, load the store with a label filter, and print the value. Then change the value in the portal, and add a sentinel refresh."),
            leo("One sentinel, many settings. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val blobSdk = Podcast(
        "az-blob-sdk",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, working with blobs in code. The SDK, metadata, shared access signatures, and leases. The SDK has three objects. A service client for the account, a container client, and a blob client. Everything hangs off those."),
            leo("How does the code sign in to storage?"),
            maya("With DefaultAzureCredential, which means no connection string in code. Locally it uses your own az login. In Azure it uses the app's managed identity, holding the Storage Blob Data Contributor role. The same code runs in both places, with no settings change."),
            leo("[chuckle] No keys anywhere. I'm seeing a theme in this course."),
            maya("It's the theme of the whole security side. Now, data about a blob. There are system properties, things like the content type and the last modified time. And there's metadata, which is your own name-value pairs, up to eight kilobytes, stored with the blob and returned on every read."),
            leo("So storing the uploader's name with the file..."),
            maya("Is metadata. Not a tag, and not a property. And one detail. Metadata keys are case-insensitive, and must be valid C sharp identifiers."),
            leo("Now the warm-up. Finding blobs anywhere in the account by a key-value pair."),
            maya("That's index tags. Here's the difference that matters. Both metadata and index tags are key-value pairs on a blob. But metadata only travels with the blob, you can't search across it. Index tags are queryable across the whole account."),
            leo("So using metadata for cross-blob search..."),
            maya("Is one of the mistakes. [pause] Next, shared access signatures, a SAS. A SAS is a URL with a signature that grants specific permissions for a specific time. A link with limits."),
            leo("And there are kinds of SAS?"),
            maya("Three. A user delegation SAS covers blobs only, and it's signed with an Entra ID credential. A service SAS covers one service, like blobs or queues, and is signed with the account key. An account SAS spans several services and account operations, and is also signed with the account key."),
            leo("Which one should I prefer?"),
            maya("The user delegation SAS. It's signed with an Entra ID key, so no account key ever leaves the vault, and you can revoke it by revoking the delegation key. An account-key SAS can't be revoked without rotating the key, for everything else that uses it."),
            leo("[sigh] Rotating the key for everything sounds painful."),
            maya("It is. There's one middle path. A stored access policy lets you revoke a service SAS without rotating the key. But the safe default is a short-lived user delegation SAS."),
            leo("Walk me through giving someone a download link."),
            maya("The API first checks the user may see the photo, because authorisation happens in your code, not in storage. It builds a SAS with read permission and a thirty-minute expiry, using a user delegation key. It returns the full URL with the SAS on it, and the client downloads straight from storage."),
            leo("And after thirty minutes?"),
            maya("The link stops working. Storage answers with a 403, and the API can issue a new one. So a short expiry keeps the damage small if a link ever leaks."),
            leo("What about uploads from a mobile app?"),
            maya("Never embed the account key in the app. That's the worst of the mistakes. Issue a short SAS from your API, or upload through the API itself. And don't give a SAS write permission when only read is needed."),
            leo("Okay. Leases."),
            maya("A lease locks a blob for writes, for fifteen to sixty seconds, or forever until it's released. You use it when several instances might update the same blob, like a shared counter or a config file. One writer at a time."),
            leo("Any catch?"),
            maya("[laugh] Yes. A lease blocks your own writes too, until you pass the lease id. People lock themselves out."),
            leo("Is there an example?"),
            maya("Two Tracks profile photos. The app asks the API for a thirty-minute read SAS. Uploads go through the API with its managed identity. Nobody ever sees the account key."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Service, container and blob clients, with DefaultAzureCredential keeping keys out of code. Metadata travels with the blob, and index tags are searchable across the account. And issue short user delegation SAS links from your API, while leases make writers take turns."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("You need the Azure CLI and the data contributor role on your account. Upload a text blob, signing in with your own login. Set metadata on it. Generate a ten-minute read SAS, as yourself, which makes it a user delegation SAS. Open the URL in a browser, and then again after it expires."),
            leo("A link with an expiry date. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val cosmosModel = Podcast(
        "az-cosmos-model",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Cosmos DB. Accounts, APIs, consistency and partitions. Cosmos DB stores JSON documents, replicates them to any region you pick, and answers in single-digit milliseconds. And you pay in request units, which are a currency for work done."),
            leo("Start me with the shape of it."),
            maya("An account holds databases. A database holds containers. And a container holds items, which are your documents. You choose the API when you create the account."),
            leo("Which APIs are there?"),
            maya("NoSQL, also called Core, for new apps with JSON documents and SQL-like queries. It has the richest features, and it's the exam's default. MongoDB, for existing Mongo code and drivers, since it's wire-compatible. PostgreSQL, for relational data that scales out. And Cassandra, Gremlin and Table, as migration paths for those kinds of app."),
            leo("Can I switch API later?"),
            maya("No. The API is fixed at creation. So if a question says \"minimal code changes from MongoDB\", choose the MongoDB API, not NoSQL."),
            leo("[chuckle] Fixed things. I sense a theme for today."),
            maya("You're right, there's another one coming. But first, consistency. How fresh must a read be? There are five levels. Strong means always the latest committed write, with the highest latency and the most request units."),
            leo("And the rest?"),
            maya("Bounded staleness lets reads lag by at most a number of versions, or a number of seconds, so the staleness is predictable. Session means your own writes are always visible to you. Consistent prefix means reads are never out of order, but may be stale. And eventual will converge, with no order guaranteed, cheapest and fastest."),
            leo("Which is the default?"),
            maya("Session. It's what most apps want. A user sees their own update immediately, and other users soon after, for far less than Strong. Using Strong everywhere is a mistake, it doubles the cost of reads and adds latency across regions."),
            leo("So a leaderboard that can be slightly late, but never out of order..."),
            maya("Is consistent prefix. Order without the cost of Strong. [pause] Now, the exam's favourite. Partition keys."),
            leo("Go on."),
            maya("Every container is split into logical partitions, by the value of one property, the partition key. Items with the same key value live together, and a logical partition can hold at most twenty gigabytes. Queries that include the key are cheap. Queries that fan out across every partition are expensive."),
            leo("And the warm-up. The key turns out to be wrong, on a live container."),
            maya("There's nothing you can do to that container. The partition key is fixed when the container is created. You'd have to migrate to a new container. That's exactly why the exam asks about choosing a key, rather than fixing one."),
            leo("[sigh] So how do I choose well?"),
            maya("Three things, all at once. Many distinct values. Writes spread evenly across them. And present in the query you run most, so it hits one partition. Owner, a user id, is often good, when most queries are per user and there are lots of users."),
            leo("What about date, or country?"),
            maya("Date spreads evenly over time, but today's partition gets hot for writes, and per-user queries fan out. And country, never. A few values, one huge partition, and writes piling onto one value. That's a hot partition."),
            leo("So, a container of chat messages, where nearly every query asks for one conversation..."),
            maya("Partition by conversation id, which puts the common query in a single partition. Partitioning by message id would spread perfectly, and make every query fan out."),
            leo("And request units? How much is one?"),
            maya("One request unit is roughly a one kilobyte point read. A query costs more, and a write costs about five per kilobyte. With provisioned throughput you set request units per second on a container or database, and pay whether you use it or not. Autoscale sets a maximum, and scales down to ten percent of it."),
            leo("And serverless?"),
            maya("Pay per request unit consumed, which suits spiky or small workloads with no steady load. So, unpredictable traffic, where cost must follow usage, is serverless or autoscale. Steady high load is provisioned. And a 429 response means you ran out of request units, so retry with backoff, or raise the limit. Ignoring 429s is a mistake."),
            leo("Example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks stores study logs partitioned by owner. Usaid's Today page asks for his logs this week, one partition, about three request units. A report across both partners fans out to just two partitions, which is still fine. If the key had been country, both of them would share one partition, and every write would queue on it."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Account, database, container and item, with the API fixed at creation. Session consistency is the default, Strong costs most and eventual least. And pick a partition key with many values that your common query includes, because you can't change it later."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("The free tier gives a thousand request units a second and twenty-five gigabytes, on one account per subscription. Create a free-tier NoSQL account. Create a container partitioned by owner. Insert two items with different owners in Data Explorer. Then select the items for one owner, and read the request unit charge in the query stats."),
            leo("Three request units of satisfaction. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    private val cosmosSdk = Podcast(
        "az-cosmos-sdk",
        listOf(
            maya("Today, Cosmos DB in code. The SDK, queries, stored procedures and the change feed. The dot net SDK mirrors the hierarchy. A Cosmos client, then a database, then a container."),
            leo("And what's the cheapest thing I can do with it?"),
            maya("A point read. Reading one item by its id plus its partition key. It goes straight to the item, rather than running a query. Running a cross-partition query where a point read would do is the first mistake on the list."),
            leo("And when I do query?"),
            maya("Include the partition key in the query, so it stays in one partition. [pause] Now, writing. Upsert writes an item, or replaces it if it exists. And patch changes named fields, without sending the whole document back."),
            leo("Okay. The warm-up. Two clients read the same item, and both write it back."),
            maya("Without anything else, the second write simply lands on top, and the first change is gone. What stops it is the ETag. Every item carries one, and it's a version stamp that changes every time the item is written."),
            leo("So I send it back with my write?"),
            maya("Exactly. You pass it with an if-match condition on the replace. If someone else changed the item first, your write fails, with a 412. Then you re-read the current item, and apply your change again."),
            leo("[chuckle] No lock at all?"),
            maya("No lock. That's optimistic concurrency. There's only a version stamp, which still has to match when the write arrives. Not a stored procedure, and not a stronger consistency level."),
            leo("Speaking of stored procedures. What runs inside the database?"),
            maya("Four kinds of server-side code, all JavaScript. A stored procedure runs inside one partition, as a transaction, so you can insert several items atomically. A pre-trigger runs before a write, to validate it or add a timestamp. A post-trigger runs after a write, in the same transaction, say to update a counter document. And a user-defined function runs inside a query, to format or compute a value."),
            leo("Do triggers just fire on their own?"),
            maya("No, and that's a trap. Triggers must be named in the request to run. They're not automatic. Expecting a trigger to run without naming it in the request options is a mistake. [pause] And everything server-side is scoped to one partition key."),
            leo("Can items expire on their own?"),
            maya("Yes, with a time to live, a TTL. You set a default on the container, and a TTL on items, in seconds, and they're removed automatically."),
            leo("Now the change feed. What is it?"),
            maya("An ordered log of inserts and updates, per partition. A function with a Cosmos DB trigger, or the change feed processor in the SDK, reads it and does work. Update a materialised view. Send a notification. Copy to another store."),
            leo("And deletes?"),
            maya("[sigh] That's the catch. Deletes are not in the change feed, unless you enable the all-versions-and-deletes mode, which is in preview. So to react to a removal, you model it as a soft delete, usually with a TTL."),
            leo("How does the feed know where it got to?"),
            maya("A lease container. It's a small container, partitioned by id, where the processor stores its checkpoints. It tracks how far each function instance has read, so two instances never process the same change."),
            leo("Can two different functions share one lease container?"),
            maya("Only with a lease container prefix each. Sharing one without a prefix is another of the mistakes. And to replay history, you set start from beginning, so every existing item is delivered once."),
            leo("Is there an example?"),
            maya("The topic's one. Two Tracks keeps a streaks document per user. Instead of recomputing it on every page load, a change feed function updates it whenever a log is written. And when two API instances read the same streak document, the ETag stops them overwriting each other."),
            leo("[laugh] So that's where the streaks come from."),
            maya("It is. Point reads for goal pages, queries inside one partition for weekly logs, a change feed function for streaks, and a ninety-day TTL on planned sessions."),
            leo("Three lines?"),
            maya("Point reads by id and partition key, and include the key in your queries. ETags give optimistic concurrency, and stored procedures give transactions inside a partition. And the change feed, with a Cosmos trigger, reacts to writes, while a lease container keeps its place."),
            leo("And today?"),
            maya("Use the free-tier account from the previous topic. Insert an item from dot net with CreateItemAsync. Read it back with ReadItemAsync, and print the RequestCharge. Run the same thing as a query without the partition key, and compare the charge. Then give an item a TTL of sixty, and watch it vanish."),
            leo("An item that disappears in a minute. Last episode, and still dramatic. See you next time."),
        ),
    )

    val all: List<Podcast> = listOf(appService, slotsAndScaling, functions, durable, containers, apim, eventGrid, eventHubs, serviceBus, appInsights, availability, redis, cdn, identity, msal, graph, keyVault, appConfig, blobBasics, blobSdk, cosmosModel, cosmosSdk)
